/*! \page mi_neuray_example_baking Example for Baking Material Sub-Expressions

<div align="right">
    [\link mi_neuray_example_derivatives Previous\endlink]
    [\link mi_neuray_examples Up\endlink]
    [\link mi_neuray_example_distilling Next\endlink]
</div>

This example shows how to bake selected sub-expressions of an MDL material into
textures or constant values using the #mi::neuraylib::IBaker interface, without
running the distiller.

\section example_baking_new New Topics
  - Baking material sub-expressions directly (without distilling)
  - Automatic discovery of all bakeable paths in a compiled material
  - Constant detection: distinguishing uniform from varying expressions
  - Baking on GPU vs. CPU: choosing a #mi::neuraylib::Baker_resource
  - Handling uvtile sequences and animated textures during baking
  - Post-processing baked canvases (normal remapping, bool scaling)

\section example_baking_descr Detailed Description

<dl>
    <dt><b>
        Baking without distilling
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        While the distilling examples (see \ref mi_neuray_example_distilling) combine
        material distillation with baking into a fixed target model, this example focuses
        solely on baking: it compiles a material instance and bakes one or more of its
        sub-expressions directly, without applying any distillation step first.<br><br>
        The example acquires #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_distiller_api — the same interface
        used for distilling — but only calls
        #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_distiller_api::create_baker() on the original compiled
        material to obtain an #mi::neuraylib::IBaker for each requested expression path.
        Compilation is performed with instance compilation
        (#mi::neuraylib::IMaterial_instance::DEFAULT_OPTIONS) so that constant
        folding and inlining produce the most efficient representation for baking.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Selecting bake expressions
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        The set of expression paths to bake can be provided in three ways, selected at
        the command line:<br>
        1. <b>Explicit paths</b> via repeatable \c --expr flags
           (e.g., \c --expr \c surface.scattering.tint). The example creates a baker
           for each path, skipping any path that does not resolve on the compiled
           material.<br>
        2. <b>Automatic discovery</b> via \c --auto. The function \c collect_bake_paths()
           walks the entire expression tree of the compiled material (see below) and
           returns every leaf path whose type can be baked
           (bool, float, color, or float vector with 2–4 components).<br>
        3. <b>Curated default list</b> when neither flag is given. A small hard-coded
           set of commonly useful paths (tint, roughness, normal, etc.) is tried, and
           any path absent from the compiled material is silently skipped.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Automatic path discovery
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        The function \c collect_bake_paths() performs a depth-first traversal of the compiled
        material's expression tree. Starting from the top-level body, it descends into
        struct and BSDF/EDF/VDF constructor arguments, building dot-separated path
        strings as it goes. A node is treated as a leaf — and its path recorded — when
        its type satisfies \c is_bakeable_type(): \c TK_BOOL, \c TK_FLOAT, \c TK_COLOR,
        or \c TK_VECTOR of float with size 2, 3, or 4. Temporaries are tracked with a
        \c visited_temps bitmask to avoid re-expanding a shared sub-expression under
        multiple parent paths, which would produce duplicate entries.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Choosing a baking device
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        The \c --baker_resource flag selects the execution device:
        \c cpu (default), \c gpu, or \c gpu_with_cpu_fallback. The chosen value is
        passed as the \c baker_resource argument to
        #mi::neuraylib::IMdl_distiller_api::create_baker(). GPU baking uses CUDA and
        requires an NVIDIA GPU. The \c gpu_with_cpu_fallback option automatically falls
        back to the CPU baker when no suitable GPU is available, making it the safest
        choice for applications that want to use the GPU when possible.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Baking uniform vs. varying expressions
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        After creating an #mi::neuraylib::IBaker, the example calls
        #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::is_uniform() to determine whether the expression is
        constant across all UV coordinates. Uniform expressions are baked with
        #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::bake_constant(), which writes the scalar or color result
        into an #mi::IData value of the appropriate type; the value is then printed to
        stdout. Varying expressions are baked with
        #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::bake_texture(), which fills an
        #mi::neuraylib::ICanvas created via #mi::neuraylib::IImage_api::create_canvas()
        using the pixel type reported by #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::get_pixel_type().
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Constant detection
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        Even a varying expression may evaluate to the same value at every sample point
        (e.g., a texture lookup driven by a constant UV offset). The example optionally
        calls #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::bake_texture_with_constant_detection() instead of
        #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::bake_texture(). When the baker finds that all sampled
        values are identical, it sets the \c is_constant output flag, fills the
        \c detected_value output, and returns without writing a texture; the constant
        value is then printed instead of saving an image file. Constant detection can be
        disabled with \c --no_constant_detection, which is useful when an image must
        always be produced regardless of content.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Uvtile sequences and animated textures
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        When an expression references a uvtile sequence or animated texture, a single bake call
        would only sample the UV range [0,1]×[0,1] at frame 0, missing the remaining
        tiles and frames. The function \c build_canvases() traverses the expression
        sub-tree looking for \c IValue_texture constants, reads the frame and u/v-tile
        layout from the associated #mi::neuraylib::IImage, and populates a
        \c Frame_uvtiles map. The outer baking loop then iterates over every
        (frame, uvtile) pair, adjusting the UV range and animation time for each call
        to #mi::neuraylib::IBaker::bake_texture(). Output filenames follow the
        \c _frame\<N\> and \c _u\<U\>_v\<V\> suffix conventions. When the user supplies
        \c --uv_range explicitly, tile and frame enumeration are suppressed and exactly
        one image is produced for the specified range.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
    <dt><b>
        Post-processing baked canvases
    </b></dt>
    <dd><br>
        Two post-processing steps are applied after a successful texture bake:<br><br>
        <b>Normal map remapping:</b> MDL represents normals in the range [−1, 1], but
        the conventional tangent-space normal map encoding is [0, 1]. When the bake
        path ends in \c .normal, \c remap_normal() iterates over the \c Float32<3>
        canvas and applies v' = (v + 1) × 0.5 to each component before export.<br><br>
        <b>Bool canvas scaling:</b> Bool (Sint8) expressions are baked as raw byte
        values 0 or 1. \c scale_bool_canvas() maps these to 0 and 255 so that the
        resulting PNG is displayed as a standard black-and-white mask.
        <!-- Workaround to prevent a layout problem after dd tags. -->
    </dd>
</dl>

\section example_baking_source Example Source

